## 1. Full one-pair physical sector and a proposed flat-angle bound

On a connected finite graph the global Gauss law fixes total charge |A|.
Within P and record number |A|+2, exactly two B sites are occupied and exactly
one of the |A|+2 occupied sites has minus charge. For each matter word choose
an integer spanning-tree flow with the required divergence. The physical
field space is the integer cycle lattice, Fourier-dual to a finite-dimensional
cycle torus. All harmonic cycle variables remain in this representation.

The magnetic operator is a finite matter matrix M(theta) with Laurent entries.
At theta=0 every path phase is one. Because H4=-2 sum S* S and unsigned paths
have positive coefficients, its entries at zero are nonpositive and dominate
the absolute sums at other angles. Proposed diamagnetic quadratic inequality:

    <v,M(theta)v> >= <|v|,M(0)|v|>
                  >= lambda_min(M(0)) ||v||².

Together with D>=0 this gives h>=delta lambda_min(M(0)). An upper trial uses
a flat matrix ground vector times a smooth normalized field packet of width g
in ALL cycle angles, including harmonic angles, with the fixed charge flows
retained. Its magnetic expectation is lambda_min+O(g²), and its electric
expectation is O(g^-2). This suggests at each fixed graph

    lim_(g->0) g² tau inf spec(h in sector N) = lambda_min(M_N(0))/4.

This trial is a variational ground-state search. It is different from the
reference input's harmonic-angle Haar state and is not asserted to be prepared
by an actual formation event. Prove the coordinate, compact cutoff, electric
